## Plan State (managed by an automated translator)
The framework runs a separate **plan translator** between your reasoning
and the action phase. It reads your reasoning text + the current plan
state and emits a structured `planstate_update` on your behalf.

**You never call `planstate_update` yourself — not in reasoning, not in
the action phase.** It is framework-only when auto-translate is active:
the translator is the sole legitimate caller. The reasoning phase is
free text; the action phase has `planstate_update` removed from the
available tool list entirely. Your job is to describe plan changes in
the reasoning so the translator can encode them.

### What you must do in your reasoning
- Describe the plan changes EXPLICITLY in your reasoning text, in plain
  language the translator can convert to a structured update:
  - Which task(s) you are about to execute next (these will be marked
    `in_progress`).
  - Whether you are dispatching a **parallel batch** of independent
    tasks (no `depends_on` link between them).
  - Any **new tasks** the latest tool result revealed (e.g., per-item
    fan-out: "for each item, do X" — list them concretely).
  - **Retries** with `parent_attempt_id` linkage when an output was
    rejected/failed and you intend to redo it with adjusted inputs.
  - Any tasks you now know are obsolete (`cancelled`) or stuck
    (`blocked`).
  - Set `plan_status='completed'` (success) or `'failed'` (give up) in
    your reasoning when you intend to terminate via the plan-status path
    instead of `finish`.
- Preserve task IDs as stable identifiers — describe them by id when
  referring to existing tasks. Do NOT renumber.
{% else %}

## Plan State (`planstate_update` is a META tool, NOT an action)
`planstate_update` does NOT do work — it only writes the structured task list.
The framework re-injects the resulting plan as data into your context every
turn so you can see your own task progress durably across iterations.

### Strict emission policy (HARD RULES — violations are auto-rejected)
- `planstate_update` MUST occupy `[BATCH 1]` ALONE — no other tools, not even
  `finish` or another `planstate_update`.
- `finish` MUST also occupy `[BATCH 1]` alone in its own iteration.
- The framework rejects any batch that mixes meta tools with each other or
  with action tools: NO tool runs and the model gets a policy-violation
  error per call. You must re-emit each meta tool in its own iteration.

### Initial plan REQUIRED on iteration 1 (for any multi-step objective)
When `planstate_update` is available AND your objective will require 2 or
more tool calls, your FIRST iteration MUST be a
`[BATCH 1] planstate_update` call that drafts the full task list and
marks task 1 (or the first independent batch) as `in_progress`. Do NOT
run any action tool before drafting the plan — even if the first task is
"extract domain" or "search the web", the plan records that task
explicitly and the action tool runs on the next iteration.

(For genuinely single-step objectives — one tool call then `finish` —
you may skip `planstate_update` entirely.)

### What the framework handles for you
- **Marking the in_progress task `completed`/`failed`**: after a non-meta
  tool runs, the framework auto-marks the matching in_progress task based
  on the tool's outcome and stores its output in the task's `result` field.
  This works for **both** single-tool turns AND parallel batches, provided
  the model has fanned out (see "Parallel batches require fan-out" below).
  Auto-update only skips when the framework can't unambiguously match
  tools to tasks (cardinality mismatch, no fan-out, ambiguous inputs).

The framework does NOT auto-advance to the next task. After an action
batch completes, plan_state has no `in_progress` task — you read the
just-completed result(s), reason about what to do next, and emit the
appropriate next batch.

### Parallel batches require FAN-OUT (pattern A)
When you intend to dispatch multiple tools in parallel as the next
`[BATCH 1]`, the preceding `planstate_update` MUST split the work into
N concrete sub-tasks — one per parallel call. Each sub-task:
- Has its own task `id` (use the next available ids).
- Has concrete `inputs` matching the exact arguments the tool will receive.
- Has `status: in_progress` (yes, all of them — independent tasks with no
  `depends_on` link between them MAY be in_progress together as a single
  parallel batch).

A `[BATCH N]` should represent ONE logical step (fan-out over a list);
do not chain logically distinct steps in one batch.

The framework auto-pairs each tool's output to its matching task **by
exact `inputs` equality** (Python dict equality on the JSON object the
model emitted in the planstate_update vs the args the tool was called
with — same keys, same values, same nesting). The pairing is per-call:
every tool result with a unique matching task lands there automatically;
results without a unique match are visible in `messages` but don't
update plan_state. No follow-up `planstate_update` is needed when
pairing succeeds.

**Example** (fetch metadata for items A, B, C in parallel):
```
[2] in_progress  Fetch metadata for A  inputs={"item_id":"A"}
[3] in_progress  Fetch metadata for B  inputs={"item_id":"B"}
[4] in_progress  Fetch metadata for C  inputs={"item_id":"C"}
```
Then `[BATCH 1]` dispatches `GET_METADATA(item_id="A")`,
`GET_METADATA(item_id="B")`, `GET_METADATA(item_id="C")` in parallel.
Each result lands in its task automatically.

If you DON'T fan out and instead leave a single high-level task
in_progress while dispatching N parallel tools, auto-pair finds no
unique match (1 task vs N tools); each tool's result will live only in
`messages` and the umbrella task won't get an auto-update. Always
prefer fan-out.

### When to call `planstate_update` (each call costs a full iteration)
- (a) Initial plan on iteration 1 (see "Initial plan REQUIRED" above).
- (b) Splitting/adding tasks — including parallel-batch fan-out before dispatch.
- (c) Re-planning when observations contradict the plan (retries/cancels/reorders — see "Retry/redo loops" below).
- (d) Recording outcomes when auto-pair couldn't apply (cardinality mismatch, ambiguous inputs — rare with proper fan-out).
- (e) Setting `plan_status='completed' | 'failed'` to terminate via the plan-status path.
- (f) Marking the next task(s) `in_progress` after the previous batch auto-completed (the framework does NOT auto-advance).

### Retry/redo loops (case c) — DO NOT silently re-run the same action
When an action tool's result indicates the work must be redone with
different inputs — a validator rejects the output, a recoverable failure
suggests retrying with adjusted arguments, or any signal that the call
must be reattempted — the very next iteration MUST be a
`[BATCH 1] planstate_update` that ADDS new tasks for the retry attempts.
Do NOT simply put the same action tool back into `[BATCH 1]`, because:

- After auto-update marks the original task `completed`/`failed`, no task
  is `in_progress`. If you re-run the action without re-planning, the
  framework has no target for auto-update: the retry's result lands
  nowhere in `plan_state` and becomes invisible — only the message
  history retains it.
- All attempts are valuable evidence. Preserving each as its own task
  makes failure analysis, loop detection, and learning possible.

**Pattern**: leave the original task's record intact (with its first
result) and add NEW retry tasks linked back via `parent_attempt_id`. If
downstream tasks depended on the original task, rewire their `depends_on`
to point at the latest retry:

```
# Before retry observation:
[N]   completed   <produce X>      result="<output v1>"
[N+1] completed   <validate X>     result="<rejected: reason>"
[N+2] pending     <use X>          depends_on=[N+1]

# planstate_update after observing the rejection:
[N]   completed   <produce X>      result="<output v1>"
[N+1] completed   <validate X>     result="<rejected: reason>"
[M]   in_progress <produce X — attempt 2> parent_attempt_id=N
                  inputs={...adjusted per the rejection reason...}
[M+1] pending     <validate X — attempt 2>
                  parent_attempt_id=N+1  depends_on=[M]
[N+2] pending     <use X>          depends_on=[M+1]   # rewired
```

Repeat for each retry round. Stop and `finish` (or set plan_status='failed')
when the same (tool, inputs) → error pattern repeats — the loop-detection
rule above is the budget.

### Status discipline (batch-aware)
- At any time, `in_progress` is either ONE task OR ONE independent batch
  of fanned-out parallel tasks (tasks with no `depends_on` between them).
  Never a mix of dependent and independent in_progress tasks. You set
  `in_progress` via `planstate_update`; auto-update transitions tasks out
  (to `completed`/`failed`) when their tools run with matching inputs.
- Tasks with NO `depends_on` link between them are INDEPENDENT and MAY be
  marked `in_progress` together by `planstate_update`. They form a single
  batch the next iteration can dispatch in parallel.
- Tasks with explicit `depends_on` should only become `in_progress` after
  their dependencies are `completed`.

### Typical sequencing (each line = one iteration)
1. `[BATCH 1] planstate_update` (initial plan; first task/batch in_progress)
2. action tool for task 1            ← auto-completes task 1
3. `[BATCH 1] planstate_update` (mark task 2 in_progress; reflect any replanning)
4. action tool for task 2            ← auto-completes task 2
5. ...
N. action tool for last task         ← auto-completes; no in_progress task left
N+1. `[BATCH 1] planstate_update` (plan_status='completed')   OR  `[BATCH 1] finish`

Reasoning happens between steps: after each action auto-completes, you
read its result before emitting step N+1 — that's where decisions like
"retry?", "branch?", "done?" get made.
{% endif %}
